      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 2879.9,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 696,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen** and **oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**, meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, water is much more than just a chemical formula. It is arguably the most important substance for life on Earth. Here is a breakdown of what water is, why it’s unique, and why it matters:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Physical Properties (The Three States)\nWater is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states:\n*   **Liquid:** What we normally drink and what fills our oceans, rivers, and lakes.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Found in glaciers, snow, ice cubes, and polar ice caps.\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Found in the air as humidity, clouds, and fog.\n\n### 2. Why Water is Unique (Superpowers of $\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$)\nWater has several bizarre and unusual scientific properties that make life possible:\n*   **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. Because of this, it acts as a delivery system, carrying vital minerals, nutrients, and chemicals through our bodies and around the planet.\n*   **Ice Floats:** Almost all substances shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water, however, *expands* and becomes less dense when it turns to ice. This is why ice floats on top of lakes—allowing fish and aquatic life to survive underneath frozen surfaces during winter.\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps human bodies from overheating.\n*   **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some bugs to walk on water) and to other things (adhesion—which is how plants pull water up from their roots to their leaves).\n\n### 3. Water on Earth\n*   **Global Abundance:** About **71% of the Earth's surface** is covered in water, mostly by oceans.\n*   **Scarcity:** Despite this vast amount, about **96.5% of Earth's water is salt water** in the oceans. Only about 2.5% is freshwater, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible for human use.\n\n### 4. Importance to Life\n*   **Biological Necessity:** All known life depends on water. The human body is made up of about **60% water**. It lubricates joints, regulates body temperature, and flushes out waste. We can survive weeks without food, but only a few days without water.\n*   **Ecosystems:** Water provides habitats for millions of species and drives the weather patterns and water cycle that nourish forests, grasslands, and agriculture.\n\n### Summary\nWater is a simple molecule with extraordinary properties. It is the lifeblood of our planet, shaping our landscapes, regulating our climate, and making the existence of living creatures possible."
